.TH MKRADIOKEYS 1
.SH NAME
mkradiokeys \- make a signer and certified identities under it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mkradiokeys
.I dir signer user ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Mkradiokeys
writes an
.B Authinfo
file per name into
.IR dir :
one self-signed signer, and one identity for each
.I user
certified by it, in the format
.IR keyring (2)'s
.B readauthinfo
expects.
.PP
Run it on a host and never on a board.  A satellite that holds a signer
key is a satellite that can mint identities, and therefore one that can
be made to trust anything; what belongs on the board is its own
identity and the signer's public key, which is what the certified
.B Authinfo
carries.
.PP
The keys are 1024 bits and the Diffie-Hellman parameters are the NIST
DSA primes, which is what
.B keyring->dhparams
gives at that size.  Generating them takes minutes.
.SH EXAMPLE
Under a host
.IR emu ,
rooted at the tree:
.PP
.EX
	; mkradiokeys /keys radio-signer ground satellite
	signer radio-signer
	user ground
	user satellite
	MKRADIOKEYS-OK
.EE
.PP
.B /keys/ground
and
.B /keys/satellite
then go into a boot image's root section;
.B /keys/radio-signer
stays on the host.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/mkradiokeys.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.I authchk (1),
.I keyring (2)
.SH BUGS
.I Emu
writes as the user named in
.BR /dev/user ,
which is
.B inferno
unless
.BI /usr/ $user
exists under the root, so a host user with no such directory cannot
write the keys it has just generated.  Make the directory first.
.PP
The certificates do not expire.
.SH HISTORY
Writing this found that
.B keyring->dhparams
could not be called at all on a 64-bit host:
.B DSAprimes
filled a 20-byte seed with five writes through a
.B ulong
pointer, which is 40 bytes where a ulong is 64 bits.  See
.BR libsec/dsaprimes.c .
